=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r8
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the OSPF virtual link between r4 (router-id 14.1.4.1) and r8 (router-id 18.1.4.1), which traverses area 1 as transit. r8 has no physical interface in the OSPF backbone (area 0); its entire backbone connectivity derives exclusively from this virtual link. The virtual link is administratively established — both routers report one full virtual adjacency through area 1 — but the area 0 LSDB on r8 is missing the Type 4 (ASBR-summary) LSA for r11 (router-id 112.1.1.1). That LSA is present on every other backbone router, including r4 itself (advertised by r1=111.1.1.1, metric 10). r4 re-originates its own Type 4 into area 1 (metric 30), which r8 does see in its area 1 LSDB, but the original r1-sourced Type 4 was never flooded across the virtual link into r8's area 0 database. Without the Type 4 ASBR-summary in area 0, r8 cannot resolve the next-hop to r11 (the ASBR redistributing EIGRP into OSPF) for any Type 5 external routes. Consequently r8 has no FIB entry for any of the EIGRP-redistributed prefixes (102.1.x.x/24, 112.0.0.0/8, 113.1.x.x/24, 120.0.0.0/8). Traffic from r12/r13 arrives at r8 via the forward path (r12 → default→r11→r1→r3→r4→r5→r8) but r8 silently drops all replies — confirmed by 100 % ICMP loss from r12 to r8's loopback 18.1.4.1. After the reported restart, the virtual link re-established its adjacency but the LSDB exchange did not propagate the r1-originated Type 4 LSA to r8. The corrective fix is to clear the OSPF process on r8 (`clear ip ospf process`) — or on both r4 and r8 — to force a fresh full-database exchange across the virtual link, ensuring the ASBR-summary LSA is properly delivered and r8 can install OSPF external routes for the EIGRP domain.
=== END ===